.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt SERIAL-GETTY 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm serial-getty
.Nd a login prompt on a serial line, once there is a serial line
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar line
.Op Ar baud
.Op Ar termtype
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr inittab 5
runs one getty per console, and a serial port is the one console that may not be
there.
.Nm
is what stands in front of
.Xr agetty 8
for those: it asks the kernel whether the port has a chip behind it and only
then execs the getty.
.Pp
.Xr agetty 8
on a port with no UART behind it does not fail the way a missing device would.
It opens fine - the 8250 driver registers ttyS0 through ttyS3 for the legacy
addresses whether or not anything answers - then blocks writing the prompt,
gives up about ten seconds later, and exits.
.Xr init 8
respawns it, and ten of those inside two minutes is exactly what init calls
respawning too fast, so the entry is disabled for five minutes and then starts
over, for the life of the machine.
Nobody sees any of it:
.Xr init 8
reports it on
.Pa /dev/console ,
which on the machine this happens to - one booted with
.Ar console=ttyS0
and no serial port, which is what the ISO's kernel command line does to a
laptop - is that same absent port.
.Pp
So the kernel is asked first.
The 8250 driver autoconfigures every port it registers and reports what it found
in
.Pa /proc/tty/driver/serial :
.Ql uart:16550A
for a port with a chip behind it,
.Ql uart:unknown
for one without.
That file is the only thing that knows the difference.
.Pa /sys/class/tty/console/active
lists ttyS0 either way, because it answers
.Dq which consoles did the kernel register ,
and registering a console does not probe for hardware.
.Pp
With no UART,
.Nm
waits and rechecks once a minute rather than exiting, because exiting is what
starts the respawn loop it exists to prevent.
A port that appears later still gets its prompt.
.Pp
Only the legacy 8250 lines can be phantoms.
Anything else - a USB adapter, a hypervisor console - exists as a device only
when it is real, so a
.Ar line
that is not
.Pa ttyS Ns Ar N
is handed straight to
.Xr agetty 8 .
.Sh ARGUMENTS
.Bl -tag -width termtype
.It Ar line
The tty, named without
.Pa /dev :
.Ar ttyS0 .
Required.
.It Ar baud
The line speed.
The default is 115200.
.It Ar termtype
What to put in
.Ev TERM
for the session handed to
.Xr login 1 .
The default is
.Ar xterm-256color .
.El
.Pp
That default is a guess about hardware at the far end of a wire, and the getty
has no way to ask, so the only question is which guess is wrong less often.
It used to be
.Ar vt100 ,
which is the safest thing that can be said about a serial line and also the most
useless: a real VT100 has no colour at all - terminfo reports
.Ql colors: -1
for it, not zero - and no
.Ql kdch1 ,
the capability that says what the Delete key sends.
Everything that reads terminfo believes it.
.Xr htop 1
draws in monochrome because it is told there are no colour pairs to allocate,
readline leaves Delete unbound because nothing claims the sequence, and a pager
asked for colour it cannot produce prints the escapes themselves.
Nothing on the other end of this port has been a VT100 in forty years; it is
QEMU forwarding stdio to a terminal emulator, or screen, or minicom.
.Pp
.Ar vt220
is the conservative choice worth knowing about: still no colour, but unlike
.Ar vt100
it does define
.Ql kdch1 ,
so Delete works.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/proc/tty/driver/serial" -compact
.It Pa /proc/tty/driver/serial
what the kernel found on each 8250 port.
.It Pa /etc/inittab
where the getty entries are.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
It does not normally exit: it execs
.Xr agetty 8 ,
or waits for a port to appear.
Bad arguments exit 2.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The shipped inittab entry:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
s0:12345:respawn:@/usr/sbin/serial-getty ttyS0 115200
.Ed
.Pp
A genuine DEC terminal at 9600 baud:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
s0:12345:respawn:@/usr/sbin/serial-getty ttyS0 9600 vt220
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr inittab 5 ,
.Xr agetty 8 ,
.Xr init 8
